  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nAn elongated cold front approaches New England today, with \nshowers and thunderstorms expected to develop ahead of it. \n\nSW low level flow increases this morning as a ridge exits east.\nThis will aid in the advection of moisture in the low levels \nthis morning, with dewpoint temps in the lower 60s forecast. \nThis will make it feel more humid today, but also provide \nadditional fuel for cumulus development and eventual showers and\nthunderstorms. \n\nSurface CAPE increases across NH and far western ME late this \nmorning. HREF members keep a wide spread of CAPE values for this\nregion, but there is more agreement that points towards the ME \nMidcoast and Kennebec Valley remaining more stable through this \nevening, thus primary focus will be points to the west. \n\nOverlapping shear is most notable in the low levels (0-1km 15 \nto 25 kts) while deeper shear is marginal (0-6km 20 to 35 kts) \nowning to weak jet dynamics aloft. There is some improvement to\nLLJ velocity into the evening, but this is when daytime heating\nwill be waning and limiting remaining storm strength.\n\nWhile these variables strike a good balance for the potential \nof some stronger storms, weak lapse rates could limit storm \nseverity and coverage. This leads to gusty winds/hail as the \nprimary threat for this afternoon.\n\nA few storms may continue into northern NH into the evening\nhours, with some isolated showers continuing into the overnight\nhours. The associated cold front will then move overhead, with\ndaytime heating Friday again initiating showers and storms\nfurther into southern Maine and perhaps the NH Seacoast. The\nmain moisture plume will have bisected the CWA, with the\nweakening front providing lift from the foothills east. CAMs\nshow some redeveloping convection in this region to the coastal\nwaters, although wind/hail threat appears lower at this time.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nThe cold front clears our region by early Saturday morning, setting \nus up for a fairly pleasant final weekend of meteorological Summer. \nDrying westerly flow out of Canada combined with a weak high \npressure cresting over the region will usher in much drier \nconditions with dew points falling to the 40s/50s. Both Friday and \nSaturday night may have strong radiational cooling, with the \npotential for valley fog development.\n\nSunday will be a bit of a transition day, with some guidance \nushering in showers to our northern zones later in the day while \nother outcomes keep it mostly dry. In any case expect a second day \nof low humidity with increasing clouds by afternoon, and the chance \nfor showers in the north, potentially becoming more widespread by \nnight. Highs will be near to slightly above seasonal averages both \ndays.\n\nLooking towards next week, the high pressure will shift south \ntowards the Southeast U.S. coast. Combined with a deepening trough \nover Canada, this puts us back into an active storm track with \nlikely a few disturbances riding on strong upper-level flow between \nthese two features. Generally expect to see increasing humidity from \nMonday onward. Temperatures are more uncertain, as 500 mb height \nanomalies start to really tick up later into next week, but \nclouds/precipitation may put a limit on temperatures.",
